# Read the docs FIRST, then keep them updated on EVERY change

## Step 0 — READ before you change anything

Before making **any** code/schema/API/config/UI/behavior change in this repo, first
**read the governance docs** so you honor the established invariants, conventions, and
history. At minimum read:

- **`docs/rules.md`** — binding invariants, conventions, security & error rules.
- **`docs/architecture.md`** — components, data flow, key decisions.
- **`docs/design.md`** — module interfaces, schemas, algorithms, API routes.
- **`docs/memory.md`** — newest-first progress log (recent decisions, fixes, gotchas).
- Plus the topic-specific docs for the area you're touching (`security.md`,
  `authentication.md`, `python-dependency-decision.md`,
  `tableau-api-capabilities.md`, `authoring-limitations.md`, `phases.md`, `prd.md`,
  `README.md`).

Do this at the start of a task (and re-read a doc if your change touches its topic).
Never contradict a documented invariant without explicitly updating that doc in the same
change and calling out the reversal.

## Then — update the docs in the SAME change

For **any** change, update the relevant Markdown docs **in the same change** — never leave
them stale. Treat docs as part of the definition of done.

## What to update (only the ones the change touches)

- **`docs/memory.md`** — add a newest-first entry describing the change; bump the
  `Last updated:` date and the passing **test count** if it changed.
- **`docs/design.md`** — module interfaces, schemas, algorithms, and the API route table.
- **`docs/architecture.md`** — components, data flow, and key technical decisions.
- **`docs/prd.md`** — product scope, user flows, functional requirements, non-goals.
- **`docs/phases.md`** — phase status/"done" criteria and the test count.
- **`README.md`** — setup, env vars, run/usage, API surface.
- **`docs/rules.md`**, **`docs/security.md`**, **`docs/authentication.md`**,
  **`docs/python-dependency-decision.md`**, **`docs/tableau-api-capabilities.md`**,
  **`docs/authoring-limitations.md`** — when the change affects their topic.

## How

- Verify facts against the code (route paths, env var names, exports, defaults) before
  writing — docs must match reality.
- Keep every doc's `Last updated:` date current when you edit it.
- In `memory.md`, newest entries go at the TOP; keep entries concise and factual.
- If a change contradicts an existing doc claim (e.g. a prior "we never do X"), fix that
  claim rather than leaving the contradiction.
- If a change genuinely affects no docs, that is the rare exception — otherwise assume at
  least `memory.md` needs an entry.
